Faster state loads: drop queued pipeline builds, no empty cache saved, a smaller upload ring

On an iPhone 14 Pro (Majora's Mask 3D, 2026-10-19) a state load took 4.3 s,
more than the boot (3.4 s). Three costs of the load, which shuts the renderer
down and builds it again (System::serialize):

- ~PipelineCache waited for every request queued on its workers. A boot
  queues a build of every pipeline in the disk shader cache
  (ShaderDiskCache::InitPLCache), each a whole Metal compile on MoltenVK, so a
  state load soon after a boot (what "Resume" does) waited for all of them
  before the new renderer could start. StatefulThreadWorker gains
  CancelPending(), which drops the tasks no worker has started; the
  destructor cancels, then waits only for the running ones. A dropped build
  belongs to the cache being destroyed.
- PipelineCache::SwitchCache saved the current driver pipeline cache before
  switching even when there was no current cache (just created after a state
  load, program ID still 0): it overwrote the title-0 file with an empty cache,
  which a homebrew program (title 0) then lost on every state load.
- The texture runtime's upload ring was 512 MiB, allocated and mapped at every
  boot and state load (0.23 s on the phone). It is a ring: one upload must fit,
  and the size bounds what is in flight. The largest single upload measured in
  play was 512 KiB (Tools/3ds-bench, Majora's Mask, 3000 frames); a 1024x1024
  RGBA8 texture is 4 MiB, 36 MiB at a resolution factor of 3. Now 64 MiB.
  Custom texture packs, which can upload more at once, are not used by the
  frontend this is built for.

--- a/src/common/thread_worker.h
+++ b/src/common/thread_worker.h
@@ -102,6 +102,17 @@
         });
     }
 
+    /// Drops the tasks no worker has started (they count as done), so that a following
+    /// WaitForRequests waits only for the ones running.
+    void CancelPending() {
+        {
+            std::unique_lock lock{queue_mutex};
+            work_done += requests.size();
+            std::queue<Task>{}.swap(requests);
+        }
+        wait_condition.notify_all();
+    }
+
     const std::size_t NumWorkers() const noexcept {
         return threads.size();
     }
